The number of girls is 3 times the number of boys, so the number of girls, $g$, is $g = 3b = 3(30) = 90$.
The number of nongendered children is 1/10 as many as the number of boys, so the number of nongendered children, $n$, is $n = \frac{1}{10} b = \frac{1}{10}(30) = 3$.
The total number of children in the classroom is the sum of the number of boys, girls, and nongendered children:
Total children = $b + g + n = 30 + 90 + 3 = 123$.

